<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giralda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Giralda is the bell tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> Cathedral, originally built as the minaret of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-seville">Great Mosque of Seville</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> in the late twelfth century. It is one of the finest surviving monuments of Almohad architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giralda.jpg" alt="Giralda" width="320" height="339" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ingo Mehling · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sevilla_Cathedral_-_Giralda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-seville">Great Mosque of Seville</a> was commissioned in 1171 by the caliph Abu Ya'qub Yusuf to replace the smaller 9th-century Mosque of Ibn 'Addabas, which could no longer hold the congregation. The Sevillian architect Ahmad Ibn Baso designed it, and craftsmen came from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>. By 1176 it was complete except for the minaret, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayer</a> was held there only from 1182. The caliph ordered the minaret in 1184 but the work halted that year with the deaths of the architect and, six weeks later, the caliph at the Siege of Santarém. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-al-mansur">Abu Yusuf Yaqub al-Mansur</a> ordered it continued, and work restarted in 1188.</p><p>Ibn Baso had begun the base in cut stone. 'Ali al-Ghumari, a Maghrebi Berber architect, built the main body in brick, and the Sicilian architect Abu Layth al-Siqilli built the small secondary shaft on top. Local brick was used with recycled marble from old Umayyad monuments. On 10 March 1198 the tower was finished with a finial of four metal spheres, to commemorate al-Mansur's victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> four years earlier.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The base is a square of 13.6 metres on each side. The main shaft is 50.51 metres tall, and the smaller second shaft above it is 14.39 metres. Inside, 35 ramps wind around seven vaulted chambers and were wide enough, one chronicler wrote, for beasts of burden, people and custodians. The facades are divided into three vertical zones: windows in the middle zone, with horseshoe or multifoil arches, and large panels of sebka decoration in the other two, above blind arcades of polylobed arches. Leopoldo Torres Balbás counted 92 reused Umayyad capitals in the tower.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> was taken by the Christians in 1248 the mosque was converted into a cathedral, and the minaret became a bell tower. The earthquake of 1356 badly damaged the mosque and brought down the metal spheres, which a cross and bell replaced in 1400. The first public striking clock in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> was added about then.</p><p>Between 1558 and 1568 Hernán Ruiz the Younger built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> belfry, bringing the tower to about 95 or 96 metres, and in 1568 the bronze weather vane known as the Giraldillo was set on top. It is 4 metres tall and weighs 1,500 kilograms, and gave the tower its name. The cathedral, with the Alcázar and the General Archive of the Indies, was registered as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1987. Other towers, among them those at Lebrija and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmona">Carmona</a>, copy the Giralda and are called Giraldillas.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giralda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Seville's <b>Giralda</b>, now the cathedral bell tower, was begun in 1184 as the minaret of the city's Almohad Great Mosque.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
